WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
The knobs that matter, and where to start.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:05.336
Most SFT runs come down to a few choices.

3
00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:13.531
The values below are starting points from documentation, not laws; each one is a hypothesis your validation metric tests.

4
00:00:13.811 --> 00:00:14.792
Learning rate.

5
00:00:15.072 --> 00:00:17.035
The single most consequential setting.

6
00:00:17.315 --> 00:00:23.629
TRL’s SFTConfig defaults to 2e-5, a typical value for updating all weights.

7
00:00:23.909 --> 00:00:31.227
For adapters, TRL’s docs suggest a higher rate, around 1e-4, because only the new parameters are learning.

8
00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:45.267
Thinking Machines (Schulman and colleagues, 2025) measured the optimal LoRA learning rate at roughly 10 times the optimal full fine-tuning rate across their experiments, and said they lack a full theory for why.

9
00:00:45.547 --> 00:00:51.179
Too high shows up as a loss that spikes or goes to NaN; too low as a curve that barely moves.

10
00:00:51.459 --> 00:00:52.781
Schedule and warmup.

11
00:00:53.061 --> 00:01:01.381
A short warmup (a few percent of steps) followed by linear or cosine decay is standard and is what the Hugging Face Trainer provides.

12
00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:07.229
Warmup avoids large early updates while the optimizer’s moment estimates are still noisy.

13
00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:08.384
Epochs.

14
00:01:08.664 --> 00:01:10.819
One pass over the data is an epoch.

15
00:01:11.099 --> 00:01:12.763
TRL’s default is 3.

16
00:01:13.043 --> 00:01:18.589
Unsloth’s guide (vendor documentation) suggests 1 to 3 to avoid overfitting.

17
00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:23.200
Small, repetitive datasets overfit in fewer passes than you would guess.

18
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:24.504
Batch size.

19
00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:31.696
The effective batch size is the per-device batch times gradient accumulation steps times the number of devices.

20
00:01:31.976 --> 00:01:42.301
Gradient accumulation lets you reach a larger effective batch on a small GPU by summing gradients over several micro-batches before one optimizer step.

21
00:01:42.581 --> 00:01:51.072
Thinking Machines found LoRA less tolerant of large batches than full fine-tuning, so don’t push the effective batch up just because you can.

22
00:01:51.352 --> 00:01:52.397
Sequence length.

23
00:01:52.677 --> 00:01:57.520
TRL truncates to max_length (1024 tokens by default).

24
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:05.736
Check your token-length distribution first: truncation that cuts off the answer removes exactly the tokens you wanted to train on.

25
00:02:06.016 --> 00:02:17.387
Maxime Labonne’s LLM course lists the same set of knobs (learning rate with a scheduler, batch size with gradient accumulation, epochs, optimizer, weight decay, warmup).

26
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:22.211
When you change one, change only that one, and compare on the same held-out set.
